  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 132 PM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nBig changes are expected the latter part of this week and into the \nweekend with increasing rain chances and a cold front. \n\nHeat remains a concern through Wednesday with above normal \ntemperatures continuing under a persistent ridge of high pressure. \nBy Wednesday, warm air advection and higher RH values may lead to \nheat indices around 110 for more than a couple of hours across \nthe eastern 2/3rd of S TX which may warrant a Heat Advisory. Will \ncontinue to monitor trends. \n\nTuesday is now looking a little more active along the sea breeze due \nto increasing moisture and a weakness in the ridge aloft, mainly \nacross the Victoria Crossroads. By Wednesday, the weakness shifts \nslightly southeast with the ridge becoming more dominant once again \nand rain chances lowering to 15-30%. \n\nA long wave mid/upper level trough is progged to deepen as it tracks \neast-southeast across the western U.S. and approaches West Texas by \nmid week. This will strengthen the low level winds across S TX and \nusher deeper moisture across the area from the gulf in the low and \nmid levels. The low will also tap into mid/upper level moisture from \nthe remnants of Hurricane Polo in the Pacific. \n\nA cold front associated with the mid/upper low is progged to move \ninto N TX on Wednesday while a pre-frontal surface trough develops \nacross S TX.  Models indicate widespread showers and thunderstorms \nby Thursday as the surface trough provides low level moisture \nconvergence and upper level moisture from the Pacific arrives \nleading to PWATs increasing to 2.2-2.5 inches. This is almost 2 \nstandard deviations above normal and is conducive for heavy rainfall \nwith flash flooding possible. This moisture will interact with the \npre-frontal surface trough and/or outflow boundaries from the \nconvection expected across the Hill Country. The main cold front is \nforecast to move into S TX Friday or Saturday with winds \nstrengthening from the north and cooler temperatures for the \nweekend. Medium rain chances continue through the weekend as the \nupper low slowly tracks east across TX. \n\nConfidence is moderate to high with respect to increasing rain due \nto the upper low and abundant deep moisture. However, confidence \nwith respect to the cold front remains low as models have been \ndiffering from run to run on timing and just how far south the front \ngoes before stalling. \n\nMinor coastal flooding will be possible starting Tuesday as winds \nstrengthen across the gulf waters. The P-ETSS indicates tide levels \nup to 1.8 feet above MSL for Tue-Thu and near 2ft by Friday during \ntimes of high tide. There is low confidence that water will reach \nthe dunes due to swell periods of 7 seconds or less, and the moon \nphase will be approaching the 3rd quarter. In addition, the wind \ndirection will play a role in how high tides get. If they are more \nsoutherly, then higher tides are less likely, but east to southeast \nwinds would be more conducive.  Will continue to monitor trends for \nany changes.",
